# ADR-001

GameFlow does not own identities or authentication. An identity provider does. MembersFlow is the current provider, not a schema dependency. Narrowed by ADR-005.
# ADR-002

GameFlow must not be tied to any union, school, foundation or company. Organizations are configurable entities.
# ADR-003

XP is shared across quizzes, crosswords, games, city activities, events and future modules.
# ADR-004

All games integrate using a standardized SDK contract.
# ADR-005

GameFlow depends on an identity-provider port, not on MembersFlow.

The domain key is identities.id. The provider and its external subject are an attribute of that identity. Adding Google, Microsoft, GitHub, or a private OAuth server is a new identity_providers row and an adapter. It is not a migration of game_profiles, scores, or achievements.

MembersFlow remains a valid provider. It is not the product boundary.
# ADR-006

Player Identity Model.

The heart of the system is not scores, achievements, or games. It is the chain IdentityProvider -> Identity -> GameProfile.

identity_providers is the catalog of authenticators. identities is the GameFlow subject. game_profiles is the player. One identity has one profile. The profile does not store a provider user id. A new authenticator, including Discord, is a row in identity_providers and an adapter. The column is code, not a MembersFlow foreign key.

An organization is context on the profile. It is not an owner and it is not an identity. A profile may have no organization.

Linking two identities to one profile is deferred. Until that decision, a second provider creates a second identity and therefore a second profile. Sharing an email or a display name does not merge players.
# ADR-007

Event taxonomy and ingestion.

An event is not free text. It is a row in event_types plus an opaque payload, the identity that caused it, and the time it occurred. Integrations may not invent type codes. Pac-Man, Snake, quizzes, crosswords, the city game, training, and surveys speak this vocabulary or they do not speak.

The read model is the envelope: event type, payload, identity, timestamp. Optional links to games or scores are not part of that contract. Recording a score does not grant XP. The progression engine does, from catalog events.

Deactivating a type stops new ingestion. It does not rewrite history.
# ADR-008

Event ingestion is a claim, not a proof.

POST /v1/events accepts provider and subject, resolves them to an existing identity and profile, and appends one active catalog event. It does not check a JWT, password, or signature. Caller authentication belongs in an adapter in front of this inlet. The endpoint must not grant XP or unlock achievements. Those are a later evaluator, reading the same events.
